  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION FOR 06Z TAF PACKAGE...\n\nThis afternoon...Skies are mostly clear except for a few marine\nlayer low clouds at the beaches. The marine layer remains about\n1400 ft deep with a strong inversion. Temperatures are near to \nabout 4 degrees warmer than at this time yesterday in the coastal \nareas, up to 6 degrees warmer in the inland valleys, 2 to 6 \ndegrees warmer in the mountains and 2 to 4 degrees warmer in the\ndeserts. High temperatures today will be in the 70s to low 80s\nnear the coast, mid 80s to low 90s for the western valleys, mid to\nupper 90s in the Inland Empire, mid 70s to upper 80s in the mountains,\nmid 90s to low 100s in the high desert, and 105-110 in the low \ndesert.\n\nNumerical guidance is in good agreement through Friday with \nrespect to the overall pattern, keeping SoCal sandwiched between\nhigh pressure to the east and a trough of low pressure to the\nnorthwest. There will be some minor differences in the forecast\ndetails but the trends should hold. We can expect gradual warming\ntrend through Thursday when temperatures will be mostly 3-7 \ndegrees above normal. By Wednesday, there is a 90-100% chance of \nat least moderate HeatRisk over the inland valleys, lower \nelevation mountains and deserts. Chances of major HeatRisk are \naround 10-30% over the far inland valleys, around 50% for the \nlower elevation parts of the high desert and 55-85% for the low \ndesert. While high temperatures won't be significantly above \nnormal, continued humidity and above normal overnight temperatures\nwill contribute to the higher HeatRisk.The marine layer will \nslowly become shallower, with night and morning low clouds and fog\nrestricted to the coastal areas for Wed-Fri.\n\nThe position of the high will bring more of a southerly flow \naloft and an increase in deep-layer monsoonal moisture for Thu \nthrough the weekend. The added moisture will bring chances for\nafternoon/evening showers/tstms, mainly to the mountains but the\npotential is there for showers/tstms in the deserts as well.\n\nAfter Friday, model solutions begin to diverge but overall it \nseems more likely than not that the high pressure will weaken a \nlittle as it is displaced farther east and south. The effects will\nbe marginal at best, with high temperatures remaining near or up \nto 6 degrees above normal on Monday.",
